This project was carried out during an internship at Prof. Howard A. Stone's Complex Fluids Group, at Princeton University, from May to August 2026. The code uploaded currently reflects the advance of the coding project as of the end of this internship.

This was my first experience with Basilisk, therefore I expect the code to not be up to the highest standards, and to be overly commented at times.

# Description of the problem

The objective of this work is to describe bubbles flowing in curved Hele-Shaw channels: while travelling through this curved arm, the bubbles are exposed to centrifugal forces, which create induced buyancy effects, bringing the bubbles closer to the inner wall of the curved channel.

However, since the bubbles also deform during their motion in these curved Hele-Shaw channels, it is unclear whether that deformation is the leading cause of the drift, a consequence of the inward motion of the bubbles, or a more complex coupled effect. Likewise, the effects of the inlet velocity and surface tension are difficult to isolate experimentally, which motivated this numerical project.

