# Modelling in Basilisk

*TODO: Add proper equations etc*

## [Pure 2D model](pure_2d/)

The first model implemented, for simplicity's sake, was a pure 2D model, i.e. solving the incompressible Volume-of-Fluid Navier-Stokes equations with surface tension, in the 2D (XY) plane of motion. Here, no effects of the Hele-Shaw geometry are implemented, only the curved channel aspect is.

## [Pure 3D model](pure_3d/)

The first model that took into account the effects of the Hele-Shaw geometry was a pure 3D model, i.e., the same incompressible Volume-of-Fluid Navier-Stokes equations with surface tension as above, but in the full 3D geometry, thus simulating the flow with the vertical confinement it has in the Z direction.

## ["2.5D" model](bretherton-brinkman-corrected_2d/)

To try to overcome the high computing costs of the 3D simulations, while still simulating the effects of the Hele-Shaw geometry, a "2.5D" model was attempted, i.e. solving the 2D incompressible Volume-of-Fluid Navier-Stokes equations with an adjusted surface tension *(add Meiburg reference, and Stone, Booth, Wu reference)*, a Brinkman correction to capture the effects of the vertical confinement on the liquid in the form of a Darcy-like friction force, and an interfacial Bretherton force meant to simulate the behavior of the advancing and retreating meniscii of the bubble, along with the effect of the thin films above and below the bubble *(Stone, Booth, Wu reference, again)*

